WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:11.710
Let's catch you up on some of the insider
stuff going on here at The Rubin Report headquarters.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:15.940
First, I should say how pleased we are not
just with the growth of the show in terms

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.360
of numbers, but also in terms of content.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:25.540
We're on track to crack 500,000 subscribers
in a few weeks and our watch time is about

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:30.859
15 minutes per video, which is almost unheard
of on YouTube.

00:00:30.859 --> 00:00:37.190
Our audience engagement is great, and even
the comment section hasn't *TOTALLY* devolved

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:40.970
into the abyss of hate like so many other
channels have.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.900
More importantly, I'm incredibly proud of
the conversations we're continuing to foster

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.040
around here.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.940
From last week's interview with Ayaan Hirsi
Ali, to the week before with Jordan Petersen,

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:54.710
and before that with Yasmine Mohammed and
Brandon Turner, I think we've managed to have

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:59.850
wide ranging, important conversations which
leave you with something to think about, instead

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:04.550
of feeling empty and enraged, like much of
the content out there is designed to make

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:06.370
you feel.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:10.670
Many of you have asked me about the YouTube
demonetization issue, which I've addressed

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:14.530
on Twitter and Facebook, but wanted to discuss
here as well.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:20.150
For those of you just catching up, about 6
weeks ago YouTube lost about 750 million dollars

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:25.310
in ad sales after a bunch of stories ran across
mainstream media, about how ads were being

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:30.430
played on offensive content on the Youtube
Platform.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:35.050
Putting aside what is actually offensive,
or that you rarely associate the ad playing

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:39.870
before the video with the content itself,
YouTube found itself in a tough position.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:44.460
750 million isn't chump change, even to a
company like Google, and suddenly there were

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.100
less ads to go around the entire platform.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:53.780
I should pause here and say that as a private
company, YouTube absolutely has the right

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:57.850
to do whatever they want with their business,
and in this case, if there aren't ads to go

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:02.770
around on all the content... well, nobody
is forcing me or anyone else to create videos

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:03.770
here.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:07.680
At the same time this demonetization was happening,
there was also another problem unfolding for

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.360
channels such as this one.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.420
Some of our videos, which deal with "certain
controversial" topics, aren't getting served

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:15.420
ads at all.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:21.080
So for example, videos we've done with people
from Lauren Southern to Bishop Barron to Yasmine

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.271
Mohammed...and there's some real diversity
in that group...aren't being served ads at

00:02:25.271 --> 00:02:27.610
all due to the nature of their content.

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:33.590
Again, that is YouTube's right to do, and
it isn't censorship because they aren't stopping

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:39.330
us from putting up those interviews, but eventually
every creator has to make a dime.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:43.800
Creators will eventually self censor if they
know certain topics won't make them any money.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:49.120
Since I try to be as transparent as possible
with you, I want to tell a little more about

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.170
our economics around here.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:57.020
Our interview with Ayaan, which was done on
location in another city due to security issues,

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:02.530
cost us over $5,000 between studio rental,
flying my crew to another city, hotel rooms

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.350
and other ancillary costs.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:12.100
The four videos we put up with Ayaan have
made us less than $1,000 back on YouTube.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:16.599
I'm no economist, but even I know that's not
a sustainable business model.

00:03:16.599 --> 00:03:22.060
In this case, not doing the interview with
Ayaan would've been the prudent business decision,

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.849
but of course I'm thrilled that Ayaan took
the time, and in my view it was worth the

00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:29.330
financial hit we took to make it happen.

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:34.200
The truth that Ayaan speaks about female genital
mutilation, the Left's alliance with Islam,

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:40.690
and the power of the individual as a lynch
pin in a free western society are all messages

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:43.470
that must be heard and amplified.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:48.900
I'll continue to have these important conversations
regardless of the financial implications as

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:50.930
long as we can do so.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:56.860
I mention all this insider stuff to you not
for your pity or your money, but just to give

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:02.660
you a little insight into how complex content
creation is these days and how there are often

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:07.520
behind the scenes issues that have little
to do with the content but a lot to do with

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:09.459
how we are able to make it.

00:04:09.459 --> 00:04:14.160
So I'll end this with a thank you to all of
you who continue to support us via monthly

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.269
pledges on Patreon or through one time pledges
on PayPal.

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:23.310
You are the ones who make it possible to do
this show and allow us to make decisions based

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:28.749
on the conversations we want to have, without
solely looking at the bottom line.

00:04:28.749 --> 00:04:32.600
We're going to continue to find new partners
to work with and other out of the box ways

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:37.460
to monetize our content, but without you we
simply couldn't do what we do around here.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:41.009
OK, enough business, let's get back to the
conversation.

00:04:41.009 --> 00:04:42.889
Joining me today is Brigette Gabriel.

00:04:42.889 --> 00:04:48.249
Brigette is the founder of Act for America,
the countries largest grassroots national

00:04:48.249 --> 00:04:50.000
security organization.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:56.099
She has a fascinating personal story of growing
up as a Christian in Lebanon during the civil

00:04:56.099 --> 00:05:01.210
war and now is a strident defender of women,
minorities and western values.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:04.069
Will our videos be monetized on YouTube?

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:04.400
There's only one way to find out...

